This is a web example, influenced by some things I discovered whilst working in Swift on DuckDuckGo’s iOS toolbar. In that work, different code paths were writing to the same view without agreeing about who owned it. I’ve written up that investigation , and it made me more attentive to what happens when an earlier instruction is still running after the state has changed.

Open a panel, close it and immediately open it again. Does it end up open?

It sounds like a ridiculous thing to need to check. But once closing involves an animation and a delayed cleanup, an earlier instruction can outlive the decision that caused it. The panel starts reopening, then disappears because the first close finally finishes.

Let’s explore that question with a disclosure panel on the web. The implementation and the bug are different from the Swift example, but the question about which instruction owns the current state carries across. Try the broken and corrected versions below.

A tempting implementation hides the panel after its closing animation:

On its own, that appears to work. But open it again before the timer fires and setting hidden = false doesn’t cancel the pending close. For a brief moment, both decisions are active. The latest click says open; the old timer still says hide.

Replacing the timer with a completion callback doesn’t automatically settle the ownership question. You still need to know whether the completed animation belongs to the current interaction. You can cancel old work or check which request owns it, and sometimes you need to do exactly that.

For this little panel, though, we can remove the delayed cleanup entirely. The content can stay in the document whilst CSS handles the visible expansion. JavaScript only needs to say whether it’s open and whether its contents can be used.

We’ll use an ordinary button followed by its content. The WAI disclosure pattern describes the button’s expanded state and its relationship to the content. This is an inline disclosure, so it doesn’t need dialog semantics or a focus trap.

< button type = " button " id = " toggle " aria-expanded = " false " aria-controls = " panel " >

< div id = " panel " class = " panel " data-open = " false " inert >

< button type = " button " id = " close " > Close preferences </ button >

The two inner wrappers have different jobs. One clips the collapsing content; the other holds the padding. Keeping padding off the grid item lets that item collapse all the way to zero.

transition : grid-template-rows 240ms ease-out ;

@media ( prefers-reduced-motion : reduce ) {

For this single-row grid, the flexible track expands to contain the content at 1fr . At 0fr , the zero minimum on the child allows it to collapse. Without min-height: 0 , the content’s minimum size can keep the panel propped open. The nested wrapper is what stops its text and controls spilling out as the row shrinks.

Changing the target during a CSS transition lets it respond from its current interpolated state. That gives this disclosure a useful reversal without measuring pixel heights in JavaScript. It does animate layout, so I’d keep an eye on performance if the panel lived inside a large, expensive page. This example favours a small amount of understandable code over a more elaborate motion system.

const toggle = document . querySelector ( "#toggle" ) ;

const panel = document . querySelector ( "#panel" ) ;

const close = document . querySelector ( "#close" ) ;

if ( ! nextOpen && panel . contains ( document . activeElement ) ) {

toggle . setAttribute ( "aria-expanded" , String ( isOpen ) ) ;

panel . dataset . open = String ( isOpen ) ;

toggle . addEventListener ( "click" , ( ) => setOpen ( ! isOpen ) ) ;

close . addEventListener ( "click" , ( ) => setOpen ( false ) ) ;

Put that script after the markup. Every state change goes through the same function, whether it comes from the toggle or the close button inside the panel. There isn’t a timer waiting to hide it later, and no…
